WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.422 --> 00:00:09.703
 Hi. Thanks, Eva, for joining us. You gave a wonderful distinguished

00:00:09.703 --> 00:00:15.934
lecture last month exploring the fertile area of games between learning agents.

00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:22.845
And you're back again this month for a deeper dive with algorithms and machine learning groups here.

00:00:22.845 --> 00:00:28.645
Very curious to hear how you trip has been going and what's bringing you back.

00:00:28.645 --> 00:00:36.073
 Microsoft Research is a great place with lots of great people. Somehow when the distinguished

00:00:36.073 --> 00:00:43.002
lecture was set up it didn't turn out to be the best time to extend for a longer visit.

00:00:43.002 --> 00:00:49.398
I'm now back mostly to work with Nikil, but I've been talking to other Microsoft Researchers.

00:00:49.398 --> 00:00:52.331
This is a great group. There are a lot of great people here.

00:00:52.331 --> 00:00:57.499
 Could you go into some high-level details about the talk. You spoke the distinguished

00:00:57.499 --> 00:01:07.257
lecture where you spoke about a new no regret model to study behavior in massive online games,

00:01:07.257 --> 00:01:13.583
like for ad auctions and traffic routing and things like that. Could you describe some of that work?

00:01:13.583 --> 00:01:18.541
 So one interesting S fact, if you think about game series from

00:01:18.541 --> 00:01:24.278
a CS perspective, that a lot of the games that we care about like,

00:01:24.278 --> 00:01:29.579
I guess, Microsoft truly must care about ad auctions, but I guess

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:33.527
another prime example is packet routing on the Internet.

00:01:33.527 --> 00:01:39.274
Those are really repeated games that are not happening once

00:01:39.274 --> 00:01:42.766
but instead happening over and over every millisecond,

00:01:42.766 --> 00:01:49.900
millions of them a day. And what's really important about them is not any single

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:58.700
act but instead the overall performance for any single packet on a packet routing,

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:05.106
if the system can deliver a packet on time, they just drop it and send it again, which works just well.

00:02:05.106 --> 00:02:09.476
The ad auctions, every single ad is a couple of, a fraction of a cent

00:02:09.476 --> 00:02:14.494
usually. And what's really valuable is the volume of ads here.

00:02:14.494 --> 00:02:18.326
In these kind of setups, the classical notions of game theory

00:02:18.326 --> 00:02:22.717
may be less applicable and it's very natural to try to use,

00:02:22.717 --> 00:02:28.363
for the participants to try to use and explore exploits style

00:02:28.363 --> 00:02:32.530
learning algorithms to figure out what might be good strategy.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:38.180
They often don't exactly know who the opponents are -- if you're a

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:43.169
packet router, you don't actually know what the other team's packet,

00:02:43.169 --> 00:02:47.958
but you can try sending it someplace and sending packets in some direction

00:02:47.958 --> 00:02:52.837
and see what happens and that's a good way to figure out what's best.

00:02:52.837 --> 00:02:57.417
Similar things apply, and the expiration is trying and seeing,

00:02:57.417 --> 00:03:00.759
see how fast it gets and then okay that was a good route,

00:03:00.759 --> 00:03:07.264
that was a bad route. Similar things happen in ad auction where there are other parameters,

00:03:07.264 --> 00:03:11.714
I guess if you're a pizza company, you do know who are the other pizza

00:03:11.714 --> 00:03:14.588
deliverers in town but you don't know what they're advertising,

00:03:14.588 --> 00:03:19.247
things change, and again trying to explore what are the good

00:03:19.247 --> 00:03:22.550
ads, what are the successful ads is a good way to learn.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:34.448
If I want to employ classical learning series here, one important facet here is that the prior,

00:03:34.448 --> 00:03:39.780
the environment in which I'm working, is not a patient prior,

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:45.348
because the other participants are also exploring learning.

00:03:45.348 --> 00:03:50.247
It's not a fixed prior. In fact, the environment reacts to me. If

00:03:50.247 --> 00:03:55.136
I start advertising very aggressively with high bids in something,

00:03:55.136 --> 00:04:00.693
the opponent might back off and go away or might compete higher. I don't know what he'll do.

00:04:00.693 --> 00:04:07.627
But it's not as steady patient prior environment, but instead something else. Maybe

00:04:07.627 --> 00:04:13.654
not the worst case but something certainly where the environment reacts to me.

00:04:13.654 --> 00:04:18.243
And in that sense this no regret learning, which is what we're using in

00:04:18.243 --> 00:04:23.302
our work, is great because that works against the worst case adversary,

00:04:23.302 --> 00:04:28.829
in particular it works with whatever this environment is. But one question I raised in the talk is

00:04:28.829 --> 00:04:40.549
that an opponent who wants to himself win the ads or route his own packet is actually not worst case.

00:04:40.549 --> 00:04:48.215
So maybe the worst case notion of the no regret theory went a little too far and the interesting example

00:04:48.215 --> 00:04:54.730
to think about that even if we are playing two person zero-sum game so every time I win you lose --

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:55.798
 Like rock paper scissor.

00:04:55.798 --> 00:05:01.457
 Rock paper scissor, matching pennies, whatever, your goal in playing

00:05:01.457 --> 00:05:07.351
this game is distinctly I guess to win which means you want me to lose.

00:05:07.351 --> 00:05:11.043
That's not the same goal as you want me to have high regret. You just

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:16.610
want to win. You're not worst case from the regret perspective.

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:21.968
You're worst case in the sense of wanting me to lose. That's not the same thing. So it

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:29.287
would be interesting to find a sort of law that understands these issues better but certainly

00:05:29.287 --> 00:05:35.917
anything that works with no regret offers certain guarantees because that's a guess,

00:05:35.917 --> 00:05:37.174
worst case opponent.

00:05:37.174 --> 00:05:41.594
 An interesting concept that came up during the talk was

00:05:41.594 --> 00:05:46.153
this idea of a course correlated equilibrium which seemed to be --

00:05:46.153 --> 00:05:53.018
at a high level it seemed to be that if we could get humans aware of their behavior, we could

00:05:53.018 --> 00:05:59.168
get them all to correlate their actions in a way to achieve a better equilibrium than what we

00:05:59.168 --> 00:06:05.534
popular know as the Nash equilibrium where everyone is selfishly looking out for themselves.

00:06:05.534 --> 00:06:15.157
I'm very curious to hear your perspectives on why you feel that's a good equilibrium to study,

00:06:15.157 --> 00:06:20.096
why the intersection of game theory and computer science naturally gives us algorithms

00:06:20.096 --> 00:06:26.831
that allow us to reach such equilibrium and how we might implement this in practice.

00:06:26.831 --> 00:06:32.498
 So early work in learning and game theory, which actually started from the econ community,

00:06:32.498 --> 00:06:36.778
not from the computer scientist, I guess maybe predating our

00:06:36.778 --> 00:06:40.922
interest in field, or predating our interest in game theory,

00:06:40.922 --> 00:06:49.300
was in that hoping that this learning players will reach a Nash equilibrium. Today we know that that's not

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:56.464
going to work out because we have computational complexity results showing that Nash equilibrium is had,

00:06:56.464 --> 00:07:01.143
in particular simple learning algorithm can't find it because no

00:07:01.143 --> 00:07:05.906
algorithm can or at least unless some complexity class is collapsed.

00:07:05.906 --> 00:07:12.882
What happens when players engage in no regret-style

00:07:12.882 --> 00:07:16.947
learning behavior is that they naturally get correlated.

00:07:16.947 --> 00:07:22.115
And the way they get correlated is that if we are playing a game

00:07:22.115 --> 00:07:26.186
together, we both are trying to learn our behavior from past data,

00:07:26.186 --> 00:07:29.399
then that past data correlates us. We're learning from the same thing.

00:07:29.399 --> 00:07:30.107
 Shared history.

00:07:30.107 --> 00:07:35.794
 We have a shared history. And that shared history causes us to do something that correlates us.

00:07:35.794 --> 00:07:42.259
So in some games, this correlation goes away and the limit

00:07:42.259 --> 00:07:44.514
and then in the end we converge the Nash equilibrium,

00:07:44.514 --> 00:07:52.376
but in other games we do not. And our behavior even in the limit will converge to something,

00:07:52.376 --> 00:07:56.876
what's called correlated equilibrium. It's an analog to Nash

00:07:56.876 --> 00:08:01.585
equilibrium except for aligning our behavior to get correlated.

00:08:01.585 --> 00:08:07.213
So one advantage of this is that it's computationally feasible like

00:08:07.213 --> 00:08:11.922
in particular very simple learning algorithms can converge to it,

00:08:11.922 --> 00:08:18.228
unlike Nash equilibrium that has computational difficulties. It also is a natural model

00:08:18.228 --> 00:08:25.731
of behavior to try to update based on data or at least I believe it is and datasets

00:08:25.731 --> 00:08:32.465
often show that people do achieve the no regret property that the learning requires.

00:08:32.465 --> 00:08:37.654
It's not clear it's a better mol, it's a reachable mol,

00:08:37.654 --> 00:08:42.284
and it's a mol that contains all the Nash equilibria.

00:08:42.284 --> 00:08:48.875
And so the Nash equilibria are particular points in the set of behaviors

00:08:48.875 --> 00:08:53.498
and course equilibria are a convex set surrounding these points,

00:08:53.498 --> 00:09:05.770
it's a bigger set. It's convex, which has advantages in finding it. Is it better? It's findable.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:12.844
And if we can say something about all of these correlated or course corrected equilibria,

00:09:12.844 --> 00:09:18.032
it says something powerful about all kinds of behavior including Nash equilibria.

00:09:18.032 --> 00:09:22.316
There is a subtle issue here. If I actually make you aware

00:09:22.316 --> 00:09:26.103
that we're playing a particular course correlated equilibria,

00:09:26.103 --> 00:09:33.527
then you might not want to play your part. It's not always or often -- it's

00:09:33.527 --> 00:09:39.648
not best response for you given that you know what I do to actually do it.

00:09:39.648 --> 00:09:46.108
You can somehow think ahead, a step ahead and be a bit smarter. And maybe I can try to be a bit smarter,

00:09:46.108 --> 00:09:55.926
too. And it's not clear what this thinking ahead behavior will do. And the reasonable

00:09:55.926 --> 00:10:00.408
models will still stay in the big convex set of course corrected equilibria,

00:10:00.408 --> 00:10:05.594
but it's more questionable that I can predict the behavior.

00:10:05.594 --> 00:10:08.629
Can I predict which set the behavior will be at?

00:10:08.629 --> 00:10:11.031
I'm not sure.

00:10:11.031 --> 00:10:18.275
 Fascinating. You mentioned the econ folks got interested in

00:10:18.275 --> 00:10:23.772
learning and game theory together before computer scientists looked at it.

00:10:23.772 --> 00:10:28.512
But I have a personal opinion, which is that you are one of the

00:10:28.512 --> 00:10:33.742
first people I look up to who started thinking computationally.

00:10:33.742 --> 00:10:37.761
My textbook that opened the world of game theory to me was

00:10:37.761 --> 00:10:41.597
Algorithmic Game Theory with [inaudible] and you and Vazirani.

00:10:41.597 --> 00:10:47.287
And I found it a very refreshing take --

00:10:47.287 --> 00:10:52.307
 Nisan. We should not forget Nisan as an actor.

00:10:52.307 --> 00:10:58.640
 I don't think I got the first edition. I got one of the editions.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.206
 It's Nisan. Always there.

00:11:02.206 --> 00:11:10.781
 My bad. Right. So could you give a retrospective on how game

00:11:10.781 --> 00:11:16.761
theory evolved and how the computational subfield algorithmic game theory evolved.

00:11:16.761 --> 00:11:19.993
 I assume you want to start by giving Christos

00:11:19.993 --> 00:11:25.108
Papadimitriou the true credit for making this evolution happen.

00:11:25.108 --> 00:11:32.951
I heard Christos talk about game theory when I first showed up in the U.S. as a post-doc researcher,

00:11:32.951 --> 00:11:38.190
I think, for me, the late '80s, maybe even earlier. At

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:42.389
that time -- so he was interested in game theory much,

00:11:42.389 --> 00:11:46.900
much before the rest of us or many of us got interested. When

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:55.629
he started to take up game theory again and when this changing,

00:11:55.629 --> 00:12:05.145
the way computer scientists think about game theory happened, is around the turn, around 2000,

00:12:05.145 --> 00:12:12.734
maybe 1999 or 8, I'm not really sure when the first talks happened. Started to

00:12:12.734 --> 00:12:20.712
champion the thought that for however long computer science existed as a field,

00:12:20.712 --> 00:12:28.185
our main mission was to design algorithms, design machines, think about how

00:12:28.185 --> 00:12:34.132
machines would work and that with the advent of the Internet this really changed.

00:12:34.132 --> 00:12:41.186
Each designing individual machines but the most important thing is how do these machines collaborate with

00:12:41.186 --> 00:12:48.688
each other and that we should think of the Internet via game theoretic thinking where multiple parties,

00:12:48.688 --> 00:12:54.182
your individual laptop and my individual laptop or a router against another

00:12:54.182 --> 00:13:01.230
router, collaborate and compete with each other in a somewhat shared,

00:13:01.230 --> 00:13:06.857
somewhat not so shared goal. The way my laptop is optimized is not

00:13:06.857 --> 00:13:11.946
actually trying to have good experience for the rest of the world.

00:13:11.946 --> 00:13:16.076
It's trying to make a good experience for me to reach the Internet and that's

00:13:16.076 --> 00:13:21.124
the same for your laptop and that's exactly what game theory tries to mold,

00:13:21.124 --> 00:13:25.420
wherever is selfishly optimizing for themselves that's what these

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:29.935
machines are doing. That's a profound change in thinking.

00:13:29.935 --> 00:13:40.700
He gave a whole bunch of talks at different conferences and really profoundly got all certainly the theoretical

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:46.318
computer science community but I think the broader CS community interested in this way of thinking.

00:13:46.318 --> 00:13:54.183
The book you mentioned, he wrote the preface for it, a beautiful preface, which I recommend everyone read.

00:13:54.183 --> 00:14:01.713
And you must read it with Noam, Vijay and Tim is wrote some

00:14:01.713 --> 00:14:03.947
chapters and asked some other people to write chapters,

00:14:03.947 --> 00:14:09.994
Christos included, so we can really, really, really fast react to the change of thinking.

00:14:09.994 --> 00:14:13.176
If one person writes a book, they will take a vow, you write all

00:14:13.176 --> 00:14:16.067
the chapters. If we ask everyone to write just one chapter each,

00:14:16.067 --> 00:14:21.458
we can put something on the shelves of everyone very, very fast and that's what we did.

00:14:21.458 --> 00:14:24.612
 You got one to correlate and collaborate.

00:14:24.612 --> 00:14:33.694
 We collaborated. But my interest in this topic really stems from Christos' convincing argument.

00:14:33.694 --> 00:14:41.827
I actually spent a sabbatical in Berkeley in 1999 to 2000 and that's when I decided to work on this,

00:14:41.827 --> 00:14:48.087
in large part prompted by Christos. So while I like the idea of bringing

00:14:48.087 --> 00:14:54.652
this, bringing computational thinking to econ or bringing game series to CS,

00:14:54.652 --> 00:15:04.613
I should give credit where credit is due. Christos was the one that really started this evolution.

00:15:04.613 --> 00:15:13.504
 In looking ahead, we're in an on line social interaction world now and an increasingly digital life.

00:15:13.504 --> 00:15:19.969
Seems like all the arguments you just described are even more vital.

00:15:19.969 --> 00:15:23.018
So do you see game theory playing a vital role going forward,

00:15:23.018 --> 00:15:24.983
like what's the outlook you have?

00:15:24.983 --> 00:15:31.748
 Definitely. You're totally right. In many different ways. One problem in applying game

00:15:31.748 --> 00:15:39.281
theory to human behavior is that it's a totally nontrivial task to model what our incentives are.

00:15:39.281 --> 00:15:47.053
In very simplistic game theory mols, you have a nice and clean objective function

00:15:47.053 --> 00:15:52.591
you say traffic routers goal is to deliver the packets as fast as possible;

00:15:52.591 --> 00:15:57.155
that humans interest is to make as much money as fast as possible,

00:15:57.155 --> 00:16:01.401
and so on. When you have a very clean objective function,

00:16:01.401 --> 00:16:07.887
then game theory offers a beautiful and elegant mol, what will happen in

00:16:07.887 --> 00:16:16.307
behavior. It's less clear if you don't actually know how to mol behaviors.

00:16:16.307 --> 00:16:23.829
So some of the examples you described are the humans involved rather than routers or automats.

00:16:23.829 --> 00:16:30.943
We have to have a better model of what do humans really want. And one interesting thing that's happening

00:16:30.943 --> 00:16:37.588
exactly part of the same digital revolution is that we have a lot of data traces of what people do.

00:16:37.588 --> 00:16:42.008
So hopefully -- and this is happening, we can infer better

00:16:42.008 --> 00:16:44.962
of actually what is a good model of human behavior,

00:16:44.962 --> 00:16:50.361
what people actually want what objective action describes them how to model their behavior.

00:16:50.361 --> 00:16:59.049
No regret learning is an attempt at such a behavior. It ignores a lot of different aspects.

00:16:59.049 --> 00:17:06.303
Certainly there's some datasets we're looking at where no regret learning is not a good behavior model.

00:17:06.303 --> 00:17:13.956
Maybe in large part because the participants don't pay enough attention. No regret learning assumed that

00:17:13.956 --> 00:17:21.669
you actually are paying attention to what's happening and updating your behavior sufficiently often.

00:17:21.669 --> 00:17:29.251
That's one issue. Sometimes there are other attention span or interest or how much you care,

00:17:29.251 --> 00:17:35.860
how often you update your bits is an important parameter that's not in these models. But there are

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:45.035
other ways that is not a perfect behavior model of what's happening even in places like ad auctions,

00:17:45.035 --> 00:17:50.034
thinking of advertisers that update very, very frequently so those are advertisers that clearly pay

00:17:50.034 --> 00:17:58.079
attention and they seem to have an algorithmic tool behind them to allow them updates many times a day.

00:17:58.079 --> 00:18:02.825
Better understanding of what drives these models and

00:18:02.825 --> 00:18:07.274
what's important will lead to better use of game theory.

00:18:07.274 --> 00:18:11.385
But I think game theory will remain an important tool.

00:18:11.385 --> 00:18:16.673
 And there's one interesting thing that came out

00:18:16.673 --> 00:18:21.894
during your talk where you commented in these ad auctions,

00:18:21.894 --> 00:18:28.706
for example, on Bing ads or something there's predictive black boxes that will give

00:18:28.706 --> 00:18:35.529
the users entire payoff curves allows users to reason about not what is the result

00:18:35.529 --> 00:18:41.237
of the action they took but also what if they had placed a different bid and so on.

00:18:41.237 --> 00:18:48.630
And I realize that having access to such predictive boxes

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:52.402
basically allows game theory to make some kinds of assumptions,

00:18:52.402 --> 00:18:58.328
but it can also lead to some frustrating thing where we don't know what's inside these black boxes and

00:18:58.328 --> 00:19:05.347
that seems to tie neatly with all our efforts in trying to make these black boxes more transparent,

00:19:05.347 --> 00:19:08.765
more interpretable and just in general there is this push

00:19:08.765 --> 00:19:12.582
within AI to make these predictive black boxes fair,

00:19:12.582 --> 00:19:18.702
accountable, transparent and curious where you see the potential

00:19:18.702 --> 00:19:22.893
synergies as we start building these models of users,

00:19:22.893 --> 00:19:27.963
how do you anticipate the interaction with game theory in the years to come.

00:19:27.963 --> 00:19:32.670
 Interesting questions you're asking and a lot of different connections

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:42.009
here. So maybe there are two parts that I want to separate a little bit.

00:19:42.009 --> 00:19:51.617
So what was relevant in the ad auction model is what do players know. So

00:19:51.617 --> 00:19:56.746
if you're a router and you chose a particular path to send your packet on,

00:19:56.746 --> 00:20:02.215
then clearly you get no feedback of what would have happened to your packet had you sent in another path,

00:20:02.215 --> 00:20:06.415
you'll have to send it static, too. So the feedback there is

00:20:06.415 --> 00:20:10.405
about the path you selected and it's not about other paths.

00:20:10.405 --> 00:20:23.625
In contrast, in ad auction, big companies, Bing included, do give better feedback. They tell the customers

00:20:23.625 --> 00:20:30.619
if they ran a bid for a particular period of time that what they would be giving you a so-called curve,

00:20:30.619 --> 00:20:36.133
giving you what other bids would have given you if you had, had you paid

00:20:36.133 --> 00:20:39.885
a different bid, then your payment would have been higher and your --

00:20:39.885 --> 00:20:47.949
but your ad would have been seen my more people. These response curves are very useful for learning.

00:20:47.949 --> 00:20:58.475
Clearly more information, it's easier to learn. And Bing, as does Google or any other company,

00:20:58.475 --> 00:21:01.568
does have enough information to give this, to make these

00:21:01.568 --> 00:21:04.741
predictive curves correct. There's no prediction here.

00:21:04.741 --> 00:21:08.888
They simply know what happened. And they can give you actual

00:21:08.888 --> 00:21:13.032
results of what would have happened had you been tired.

00:21:13.032 --> 00:21:17.762
They know what everybody else bid, they simply know what the bids were

00:21:17.762 --> 00:21:20.854
and they can aggregate the numbers and tell us what the answer is.

00:21:20.854 --> 00:21:29.754
Now, one issue they're going to raise during the talk is two advertisers trusted these curves are correct.

00:21:29.754 --> 00:21:35.731
And I have no information whether people trust it on the Bing website. But

00:21:35.731 --> 00:21:40.770
I do understand that there are certainly trust issues at other places.

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:45.409
Especially there are trust issues if you not only want to

00:21:45.409 --> 00:21:50.208
give them past data, which is a fact you should know,

00:21:50.208 --> 00:21:55.127
like Bing should know. But instead want to give a suggestion of what might be a good bid.

00:21:55.127 --> 00:22:01.871
So-called recommendation, a recommended action. There are certainly trust issues and many bidders

00:22:01.871 --> 00:22:09.400
positing and some other places prefer to use third-party optimizers due to lack of trust I believe.

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:18.349
Who knows if Bing may be really optimizing for me or are you optimizing for Bing itself, for the company.

00:22:18.349 --> 00:22:22.699
And so there is the version whether I trust the data that you

00:22:22.699 --> 00:22:26.132
provided is correct. And that might be an issue also.

00:22:26.132 --> 00:22:32.497
A bigger issue is do I trust your recommendation. And then there is the

00:22:32.497 --> 00:22:38.244
third issue that your recommendation might be based on machine learning,

00:22:38.244 --> 00:22:43.358
which we don't fully understand, and it would be nice to have a better understandable mol.

00:22:43.358 --> 00:22:50.506
Maybe they would have the trust issue, but part of the trust issue is --

00:22:50.506 --> 00:22:51.604
 Incentive.

00:22:51.604 --> 00:22:59.067
 -- incentive compatibility issue. If we're playing chess and I suggest

00:22:59.067 --> 00:23:02.958
that you know you really should make that move but you're playing against me,

00:23:02.958 --> 00:23:10.261
you might think is she suggesting a move that's good for me or good for her. So in this extent there is a

00:23:10.261 --> 00:23:16.906
trust issue that's not about the lack of understanding of what causes machine learning to make a suggestion,

00:23:16.906 --> 00:23:24.788
but instead just literally in some way we're, our incentives are aligned but in other ways they're not.

00:23:24.788 --> 00:23:27.831
So there's possibly an incentive problem.

00:23:27.831 --> 00:23:31.064
 Does the -- I'm sure for anyone who is looking

00:23:31.064 --> 00:23:35.484
up your work and others' work in algorithmic game theory,

00:23:35.484 --> 00:23:42.263
they'll see the phrase mechanism design come up often. Could you explain at a high level what that

00:23:42.263 --> 00:23:52.066
is and could some ideas from there help with this incentive alignment problem and engender trust?

00:23:52.066 --> 00:23:56.013
 Sure. Algorithmic design at a high level is

00:23:56.013 --> 00:24:00.910
designing algorithms that would be good for selfish users.

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:08.429
You know, the prototypical classic example is auctioning if, we're auctioning off

00:24:08.429 --> 00:24:18.478
a single item and we could auction it the way you contract for selling a house,

00:24:18.478 --> 00:24:21.779
namely you make an offer and then the highest offer will get

00:24:21.779 --> 00:24:25.351
taken, and it's going to have to pay whatever he offered,

00:24:25.351 --> 00:24:28.783
in which case there's a lot of strategizing going into what might be the

00:24:28.783 --> 00:24:33.056
right bid, you want to get the house but you would like to get it cheap.

00:24:33.056 --> 00:24:37.982
And then the second prize where again the highest bidder

00:24:37.982 --> 00:24:41.863
wins but the price you pay is the second highest bid.

00:24:41.863 --> 00:24:45.714
That is the minimum bid he had to make in order to win, not

00:24:45.714 --> 00:24:50.563
his actual bid. But the smallest number he could have had,

00:24:50.563 --> 00:24:56.530
which effectively does the strategizing for you and it's easy to think through that and the second price

00:24:56.530 --> 00:25:05.358
it's best to just tell the true maximum you're willing to pay because that's not going to be the price.

00:25:05.358 --> 00:25:13.849
Algorithm design is to offer names that are trying to design algorithms where

00:25:13.849 --> 00:25:20.465
the simple truthful behavior is everyone's best behavior in the system,

00:25:20.465 --> 00:25:28.787
no matter what everyone else does, which certainly makes life easier. One issue is

00:25:28.787 --> 00:25:35.761
using algorithm design in the kind of situations I talked about in the lecture,

00:25:35.761 --> 00:25:45.608
that is theory that I repeated, that you have to be careful it stays -- sorry, it stays a good --

00:25:45.608 --> 00:25:49.390
a good strategy for people to tell the truth even in the

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:53.762
repeated setting. For example, in a second price auction,

00:25:53.762 --> 00:26:02.610
it does not. I tell you an iteration what my true value for this ad is about ten seconds

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:07.469
later the reserve price will go up just a second below my true value and look what,

00:26:07.469 --> 00:26:13.176
suddenly I'm going to have to tell my value, pay my value. That's not going to be so good.

00:26:13.176 --> 00:26:22.415
So there's a beautiful theory of single shot mechanisms that truth telling is a dominant strategy,

00:26:22.415 --> 00:26:29.129
that's a good behavior for people. And we only now started to develop the area

00:26:29.129 --> 00:26:33.829
called dynamic mechanism design that wants to develop good mechanisms that

00:26:33.829 --> 00:26:39.801
stay good even in repeated setting design good that's in repeated setting.

00:26:39.801 --> 00:26:44.460
Patti Bernard: Popping up from the technical level, I wanted

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:48.246
to ask meta level questions about the research process.

00:26:48.246 --> 00:26:55.360
For people who are interested in game theory and were getting started reading up on the literature,

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:59.093
the vast fascinating literature and exciting results in dynamic

00:26:59.093 --> 00:27:05.557
mechanisms design, it can be intimidated throwing acronyms like STOC,

00:27:05.557 --> 00:27:15.125
FOX, EC, SODA. I'm curious, could you elaborate a bit on the community, a bit on the research process,

00:27:15.125 --> 00:27:18.747
what it takes to make an impactful contribution.

00:27:18.747 --> 00:27:24.204
 So I guess these acronyms you're throwing around are conference names. And in fact,

00:27:24.204 --> 00:27:32.855
interestingly, these are theory conference names. Despite this being an AI distinguished series,

00:27:32.855 --> 00:27:40.547
I more identified as a member of the theory community. Although in the economics and computing space,

00:27:40.547 --> 00:27:47.317
there's a lot of interaction between AI and theory. The first talk is two

00:27:47.317 --> 00:27:52.929
theory conferences and a lot of theoretical work or some amount of theoretical

00:27:52.929 --> 00:28:01.235
work in this game theory economics interface does get published in FOX SOC.

00:28:01.235 --> 00:28:10.549
EC, which stands for EC, Conference of Economics and Computation is a dedicated conference

00:28:10.549 --> 00:28:18.052
to this topic and in fact it's a conference that has a multiple different tracks,

00:28:18.052 --> 00:28:27.291
has a theory track, has an AI track and has an empirical track, applied track. I forgot, empirical track.

00:28:27.291 --> 00:28:33.467
And indeed the conference, the regular conference attended, there's a lot of theoreticians,

00:28:33.467 --> 00:28:38.761
a lot of people from the AI community and even some people

00:28:38.761 --> 00:28:42.087
from the econ community regularly show up and some,

00:28:42.087 --> 00:28:47.076
they work at the EC. So EC has been a really great fertile

00:28:47.076 --> 00:28:55.596
ground of mixing at the interface of AI and theory even.

00:28:55.596 --> 00:29:04.107
Some amount of machine learning, though, actually more classical AI and the AI people like Ariel Procaccia,

00:29:04.107 --> 00:29:13.765
or David Parkes or Santomer or a whole bunch of them that are really interested. Shorm. There's

00:29:13.765 --> 00:29:21.108
a long list of lead AI researchers who are very interested in the game theory interface.

00:29:21.108 --> 00:29:27.414
Often these people don't literally ask the same questions, but they're very related questions and very --

00:29:27.414 --> 00:29:41.452
it's a really great community where people interact with each other. You asked how it makes an impact,

00:29:41.452 --> 00:29:47.803
I mean the way that computer science and many of the fields science work,

00:29:47.803 --> 00:29:52.447
the apprenticeship system getting Ph.D. students to work with an advisor.

00:29:52.447 --> 00:30:03.303
And I definitely think that the way, as you point out, the literature is vast and just someone

00:30:03.303 --> 00:30:08.221
completely on their own getting started and reading literature seem like a very hard task,

00:30:08.221 --> 00:30:14.667
and I certainly wouldn't recommend this to people and in fact having some guidance

00:30:14.667 --> 00:30:21.212
with someone senior in the field I think is the right way to get started.

00:30:21.212 --> 00:30:29.603
And I guess one way this person can help you or maybe you can realize this, that some of the changes

00:30:29.603 --> 00:30:35.839
in life and some of the changes in perspective because of our computer science perspective,

00:30:35.839 --> 00:30:45.287
changes the kind of questions we ask. And working on those questions is incredibly impactful because these

00:30:45.287 --> 00:30:54.855
are new questions and also maybe a little bit easier to make an impact because often you want to --

00:30:54.855 --> 00:30:59.443
you realize that, yes, there is an amazing literature out there with a lot of beautiful work,

00:30:59.443 --> 00:31:04.005
but this is a new question. Like, for example, I was

00:31:04.005 --> 00:31:07.124
originally thinking of [inaudible] has a question like this.

00:31:07.124 --> 00:31:12.036
There's a lot of beautiful work in classical game theory literature on when is

00:31:12.036 --> 00:31:17.213
it that Nash equilibrium is guaranteed to have socially optimized outcome,

00:31:17.213 --> 00:31:20.415
when is it that it doesn't have socially optimized outcome. But they

00:31:20.415 --> 00:31:23.800
much less ask the question when it's not actually literally optimal.

00:31:23.800 --> 00:31:31.099
How often is it. And it does make a difference if the welfare equilibrium is

00:31:31.099 --> 00:31:36.241
vanishing and you're in a tragedy of the commons kind of situation versus,

00:31:36.241 --> 00:31:41.070
yeah, we lost a little bit, it's not so bad. And making that distinction is important

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:47.598
and it's a question that has not been extensively studied historically in economics.

00:31:47.598 --> 00:31:55.777
Patti Bernard: So asking these new questions based on our new perspectives can lead to impactful research.

00:31:55.777 --> 00:32:02.386
 I think so. And I think the combination of learning and games is also a bit like this.

00:32:02.386 --> 00:32:09.476
Even though there's a history of learning of games and combination, again, I think the econ

00:32:09.476 --> 00:32:15.672
focus was what is the kind of learning behavior that helps the players find the Nash equilibrium

00:32:15.672 --> 00:32:21.838
and that kind of preplay or cheap talk before the game on which record it on Nash equilibrium

00:32:21.838 --> 00:32:28.652
and I guess our perspective is the big data is thinking of learning as a behavior model.

00:32:28.652 --> 00:32:32.553
This is what people do, not to find an equilibrium, this

00:32:32.553 --> 00:32:35.218
is just real life, and I want to know what happens.

00:32:35.218 --> 00:32:42.710
Patti Bernard: Right. And for students who have got started on research or early stage career researchers,

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:45.905
very curious if you have any advice on your organizational

00:32:45.905 --> 00:32:50.892
principles for research or how do you balance demands on your time.

00:32:50.892 --> 00:32:57.976
 Demands on my time. I wish I balanced it better. And there are lots of things you want

00:32:57.976 --> 00:33:04.147
to do and I guess you have to figure out what works for you for protecting some of your time.

00:33:04.147 --> 00:33:11.411
I really like teaching. I like interacting with people who don't yet know

00:33:11.411 --> 00:33:16.295
the field and showing them the beauty in algorithms in game theory.

00:33:16.295 --> 00:33:26.816
But at the same time I also want to have enough impact in research. As you get older, you get asked to

00:33:26.816 --> 00:33:32.882
do a fair amount of service and there's an impact to the community and I guess you care about that,

00:33:32.882 --> 00:33:40.235
too. And of course all of us have some personal life. So this is not even for 24 hours but less than that,

00:33:40.235 --> 00:33:45.294
because you might want to do other things in your life. It's a hard task and you

00:33:45.294 --> 00:33:50.681
have to find your own balance of what might be right and what's important.

00:33:50.681 --> 00:33:55.306
I don't know if I have really good advice. Patti Bernard: Any good

00:33:55.306 --> 00:34:00.273
habits for being productive and asking impactful questions and research?

00:34:00.273 --> 00:34:05.907
Any daily habits that you have that you think --

00:34:05.907 --> 00:34:09.489
 One thing that, especially since I'm doing this at Microsoft, and

00:34:09.489 --> 00:34:14.153
maybe some of the audience are Microsoft or other industry researchers,

00:34:14.153 --> 00:34:23.298
is I find the pressure coming from the National Science Foundation to write grant proposals very helpful.

00:34:23.298 --> 00:34:30.777
It makes one think every couple of years how to pitch forth research not an

00:34:30.777 --> 00:34:35.659
existing result that you already proved and you want to explain to a conference,

00:34:35.659 --> 00:34:40.718
but in a way gets you involved in to effectively writing the

00:34:40.718 --> 00:34:43.631
introduction in your paper before you prove the paper;

00:34:43.631 --> 00:34:47.941
that is, before you know what results you have you need to try

00:34:47.941 --> 00:34:51.723
to explain to someone that you will care about the results.

00:34:51.723 --> 00:34:58.148
This is very important. You need to be able to convince yourself that what you're doing will have an impact

00:34:58.148 --> 00:35:03.027
and it's important and it's a good idea to try to write this down and try to convince the community of

00:35:03.027 --> 00:35:11.787
this and engage in this thinking possibly before you spend months and months figuring out the answer.

00:35:11.787 --> 00:35:18.671
I don't know what replaces this if you're an industry researcher, but as an academic,

00:35:18.671 --> 00:35:24.568
NSF is making me or NSF and other funding agencies making me write grant proposals.

00:35:24.568 --> 00:35:29.846
And as annoying as it can be when the deadline pressure happens, at the high level

00:35:29.846 --> 00:35:36.631
it's really great that people are engaging in this high level thinking of what --

00:35:36.631 --> 00:35:44.732
I also like the idea of somewhat going with the flow and what are new and interesting questions and

00:35:44.732 --> 00:35:51.048
keeping your eyes open of what are the research areas which are new and exciting and where the kind

00:35:51.048 --> 00:35:57.882
of knowledge and tools that you bring to research could be impactful is something I would like to --

00:35:57.882 --> 00:36:01.584
I did in my past. I hope I did in my past and I suggest

00:36:01.584 --> 00:36:07.130
everyone else should be open to somewhat changing areas.

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:15.073
Patti Bernard: As we conclude the chat, I'm curious to hear

00:36:15.073 --> 00:36:18.206
your outlook not only on technical side of game theory,

00:36:18.206 --> 00:36:24.362
what interesting questions are out there, but how we might see it implemented in society or in applications,

00:36:24.362 --> 00:36:31.715
like what's the role of government, what's the role of private actors and how might this play out.

00:36:31.715 --> 00:36:38.510
 It's a very hard question. I'm not so sure I know how to react to this. In

00:36:38.510 --> 00:36:46.232
some way what government and the rules of government do is a form of mechanism design.

00:36:46.232 --> 00:36:49.435
You can interfere with what's allowed and not allowed and what

00:36:49.435 --> 00:36:54.085
are the ways we're interacting with each other as a society.

00:36:54.085 --> 00:37:03.154
In places like ad auction companies and doing mechanism design also they're designing their mechanism but

00:37:03.154 --> 00:37:11.265
there is a higher level version where Microsoft competes with Google or Facebook or whatever the competitors

00:37:11.265 --> 00:37:20.475
and that mechanism design is governed by rules of the country or legal laws and stuff like that.

00:37:20.475 --> 00:37:28.918
So that's a mechanism design also. So there are a lot of high level questions of this form.

00:37:28.918 --> 00:37:39.801
Game theory, there's the sort of game theory of the kind I more talked about in the lecture

00:37:39.801 --> 00:37:45.238
whose goal is more trying to understand what likely happens in the outcomes of the game,

00:37:45.238 --> 00:37:48.451
that understanding is definitely helpful in designing rules. You

00:37:48.451 --> 00:37:53.875
want to know what will happen before you implement a rule change.

00:37:53.875 --> 00:38:00.374
Mechanism design is directly aiming at designing mechanisms with the goal

00:38:00.374 --> 00:38:06.749
of making social effort high or making inequality less bad and many other

00:38:06.749 --> 00:38:12.456
great goals that hopefully our companies and governments aim to implement.

00:38:12.456 --> 00:38:19.121
I sincerely hope that a lot of the research that's happening here will help companies

00:38:19.121 --> 00:38:27.323
have better mechanisms in their own space and also advice governments in some way.

00:38:27.323 --> 00:38:32.730
That's the ultimate goal. Patti Bernard: I wanted to spend at least a sentence championing

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:40.104
your selfless community service and writing books and like selfless education,

00:38:40.104 --> 00:38:45.990
your involvement with ACM and outreach efforts. I wanted to give you a

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:53.114
chance to like celebrate and amplify the work that you're doing in all

00:38:53.114 --> 00:39:00.008
these other areas that make the community a worthwhile place to be a part of.

00:39:00.008 --> 00:39:07.157
 Thank you very much. Writing books -- I guess there's two books associated with me

00:39:07.157 --> 00:39:14.577
the Algorithms Textbook with Jon Kleinberg and this Algorithmic Game Theory edited volume.

00:39:14.577 --> 00:39:23.443
For both of them I got involved and I did it because I had a view of what I want

00:39:23.443 --> 00:39:29.129
to tell the community and the book is an excellent way to have an impact.

00:39:29.129 --> 00:39:37.726
Algorithms, of course, is taught at every school and required at many, many schools and

00:39:37.726 --> 00:39:48.678
we had a way of wanting to teach the course differently than many other textbooks.

00:39:48.678 --> 00:39:58.894
I guess one important aspect is to emphasize throughout the course how important these applications are,

00:39:58.894 --> 00:40:07.056
rather than starting a lecture with X1 and XN are numbers and you want to do this. Every chapter,

00:40:07.056 --> 00:40:13.387
every lecture starts with some application that explains what on the earth this X might be

00:40:13.387 --> 00:40:21.124
and we're assigning [inaudible] to this way instead of it starting with given the numbers,

00:40:21.124 --> 00:40:24.736
find the median. It's some sort of English story, the

00:40:24.736 --> 00:40:27.749
company wants this or that. If you understood the story,

00:40:27.749 --> 00:40:33.975
at the heart of it you actually learn the median. But the first

00:40:33.975 --> 00:40:37.347
job for an undergraduate is to understand what the English said,

00:40:37.347 --> 00:40:41.577
what did this company really want. I think this is very important, maybe

00:40:41.577 --> 00:40:45.389
one of the most important things we're teaching as part of algorithms.

00:40:45.389 --> 00:40:48.946
And we wanted to change the course and change the way

00:40:48.946 --> 00:40:53.441
algorithms is taught in many, many schools to emphasize this,

00:40:53.441 --> 00:40:57.367
I guess, often called the modeling aspect. And so we

00:40:57.367 --> 00:41:02.942
wrote a textbook that does this. I also really liked --

00:41:02.942 --> 00:41:08.506
and again that's another thing we do both in lectures and in the book -- is not start with

00:41:08.506 --> 00:41:14.463
the correct algorithm but start with the first natural ID and have a lecture or in the

00:41:14.463 --> 00:41:21.377
textbook explain of how this would be a natural thing to do but it doesn't quite work at.

00:41:21.377 --> 00:41:26.485
You have to do a more sophisticated thing, that is to lead people how someone

00:41:26.485 --> 00:41:31.209
saw this algorithm rather than just here's a beautiful algorithm done.

00:41:31.209 --> 00:41:38.847
Again, that was the way I like thinking and I wanted to think, and I wanted to teach this way at Cornell.

00:41:38.847 --> 00:41:45.782
And I love the idea that many other places adopted either the book or even when they didn't adopt the book,

00:41:45.782 --> 00:41:51.878
they adopted this way of thinking. Algorithmic Game Theory is a bit similar. It was a new field.

00:41:51.878 --> 00:42:00.528
We wanted to get many people involved. We wanted to get the knowledge out there. And I don't know,

00:42:00.528 --> 00:42:05.616
I think having impact through this getting knowledge of getting a

00:42:05.616 --> 00:42:08.769
way of thinking out there is part of what happens in teaching,

00:42:08.769 --> 00:42:14.337
too. I did say I like teaching. I like bringing people who don't yet know anything

00:42:14.337 --> 00:42:19.485
and bringing them algorithms or bringing them game theory or any field I know.

00:42:19.485 --> 00:42:23.496
And a book did a little bit the same thing. So it serves the same way

00:42:23.496 --> 00:42:27.996
of having impact. Patti Bernard: You remain an inspiration for me.

00:42:27.996 --> 00:42:32.923
My introduction to computer science was through computer design [inaudible].

00:42:32.923 --> 00:42:38.023
My first brush with serious computer science was a machine learning project

00:42:38.023 --> 00:42:42.952
which crucially needed a very fast network flow algorithm as a subroutine.

00:42:42.952 --> 00:42:49.470
Your network for algorithm. My first textbook in graduate school was Algorithm Game Theory.

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:55.839
And I think throughout grad school I was so fortunate to have you as my advisor, too.

00:42:55.839 --> 00:43:00.941
 Thank you very much. Patti Bernard: You have been an inspiration throughout and you remain,

00:43:00.941 --> 00:43:05.899
and you will remain, and I'm sincerely thankful. But I also want to thank --

00:43:05.899 --> 00:43:08.274
 I want to add you were a great student to have,

00:43:08.274 --> 00:43:14.470
because you're a more empirical machine learning student,

00:43:14.470 --> 00:43:21.704
not on your own publishing naturally in a FOX talk community yet you seem very

00:43:21.704 --> 00:43:30.903
interested in theoretical ideas and more willing to think what impact they have in AI.

00:43:30.903 --> 00:43:36.859
And I think people, in order for these two fields to successfully interact with each other,

00:43:36.859 --> 00:43:44.232
we need both sides. We need people with theoretical interests and who identify as

00:43:44.232 --> 00:43:49.919
theoreticians to be interested in having an impact but we need people from the more

00:43:49.919 --> 00:43:54.788
empirical community to actually be interested in reaching out and using these ideas.

00:43:54.788 --> 00:43:57.722
And it's great to have people like you. Patti Bernard: Thanks

00:43:57.722 --> 00:44:01.733
so much, Eva. And thanks from all of MSR for visiting us again,

00:44:01.733 --> 00:44:04.367
and we're looking forward to many more such visits.

00:44:04.367 --> 00:44:12.970
 Thank you. It's a great place to visit.

